\chapter{\texorpdfstring{Parser Combinators: \texttt{ParsecInput},
\texttt{ExpectedResult},
\texttt{Parser}}{Parser Combinators: , , }}\label{ch23-parser-combinators-parsecinput-expectedresult-parser}

MuDSL ships a small parser-combinator core in the \texttt{dsl}
namespace, organized around three abstractions: an input cursor
(\texttt{ParsecInput}), a structured result type
(\texttt{ExpectedResult\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}),
and a callable model for parsers
(\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}).
This chapter documents those three primitives and the machinery that
ties them together. The combinators themselves --- sequence,
alternative, repetition, optional --- are the subject of Chapter 24;
this chapter concerns only the substrate on which they are built.

The design is deliberately Parsec-like. A parser is not a function that
throws on failure; it is a value, a callable that takes a mutable cursor
by reference and returns a result object. Failure is a value, not an
exception. This mirrors the philosophy of
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
introduced in Chapter 22, and readers familiar with that type will
recognize the shape of the error flow immediately. The parser core
reuses the same discipline: errors are carried alongside values,
accumulated best-effort, and never raised through the call stack.

This chapter is prerequisite for Chapter 24 (primitive parsers and the
basic combinators), Chapter 25 (diagnostics, semantic actions, and the
full \texttt{run\_\allowbreak{}parser} machinery), and the PEG layers of
Chapter 27 and Chapter 28. The token-level integration sketched in
\texttt{examples/\allowbreak{}15-tokenize-pars\allowbreak{}e-integration.\allowbreak{}cpp}
also rests on the types documented here.

\section{The Parser-Combinator Core}\label{ch23-the-parser-combinator-core}

The header overview describes the combinator subsystem concisely.

\begin{code}
 * --- CombinatorParser ---
 *
 *   Functional parser core + combinators (`|`, `&`, `*`, optional()).
 *
 *   Usage:
 *     auto p = dsl::parser([](dsl::ParsecInput&
 * in)->dsl::ExpectedResult<char>{...});
 *
 *   Example:
 *     auto seq = p1 & p2;
\end{code}

Three observations follow from this summary. First, parsers are
constructed by wrapping a lambda with
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}parser}, which deduces the value
type from the lambda's return type. Second, a parser's lambda takes
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}ParsecInput\&} and returns
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}ExpectedResult\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
Third, parsers compose with operators --- \texttt{|}, \texttt{\&},
\texttt{*} --- into larger parsers; those operators are documented in
Chapter 24 and are mentioned here only to motivate why the core types
exist.

The core is small enough to read in one sitting. \texttt{ParsecInput} is
a struct with two fields and four member functions. \texttt{ParseError}
is a struct with three fields and no member functions.
\texttt{ExpectedResult\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
is a struct pairing an
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
with a \texttt{ParseError}.
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is a
thin wrapper around \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}function}.
The entire combinator library is built on these four types plus a
handful of free-function templates.

The rest of this chapter walks through each type in turn, in the order a
parser author encounters them: first the input cursor, then the error
record, then the result type, then the parser model, and finally the
\texttt{run\_\allowbreak{}parser} entry point.

\section{ParsecInput: The Input
Cursor}\label{ch23-parsecinput-the-input-cursor}

\texttt{ParsecInput} is the mutable input state passed to every parser
invocation. It tracks the source text and a cursor position, and it
exposes the primitive operations that low-level parser callables use to
inspect and consume input.

\begin{code}
struct ParsecInput
{
  std::string_view source{};
  std::size_t pos{ 0 };
  char
  peek () const
  {
    return pos < source.size () ? source[pos] : '\0';
  }
  bool
  eof () const
  {
    return pos >= source.size ();
  }
  char
  consume ()
  {
    return eof () ? '\0' : source[pos++];
  }
  std::string_view
  get_span (std::size_t start) const
  {
    return source.substr (start, pos - start);
  }
};
\end{code}

The struct is an aggregate with default-initialized members.
\texttt{source} is a non-owning \texttt{string\_\allowbreak{}view}; the
caller is responsible for keeping the underlying storage alive for the
duration of the parse. \texttt{pos} is a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}size\_\allowbreak{}t} index into
\texttt{source}, defaulting to zero. There is no separate end pointer;
the cursor's extent is always
\texttt{source.\allowbreak{}size(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}.

Because \texttt{ParsecInput} is an aggregate, it can be
value-initialized with brace syntax.
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}run\_\allowbreak{}parser}
constructs one internally as
\texttt{ParsecInput\{ sou\allowbreak{}rce,\allowbreak{} 0 \}}. User code
that needs to drive a parser by hand --- for example, to parse a prefix
and then continue manually --- can construct a \texttt{ParsecInput} the
same way.

The cursor is passed to parsers by reference. A parser that succeeds
typically advances \texttt{pos} by one or more positions; a parser that
fails is expected to leave \texttt{pos} where it found it, or to be
wrapped by a combinator that restores it. The combinators in Chapter 24
enforce the save-and-restore discipline; a hand-written parser must
respect it manually.

Note that there is no \texttt{remaining(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
member and no \texttt{advance(\allowbreak{}n)\allowbreak{}} member.
Input is consumed one character at a time through
\texttt{consume(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}, and the remaining suffix
is always available as
\texttt{source.\allowbreak{}substr(\allowbreak{}pos)\allowbreak{}}
should a parser need it. The public \texttt{pos} member also permits
direct index arithmetic, which the combinators use for save/restore.

\section{Position, Peeking, and End of
Input}\label{ch23-position-peeking-and-end-of-input}

\texttt{peek(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} returns the character at the
current cursor position without advancing. If the cursor is at
end-of-input, it returns \texttt{'\textbackslash{}0'}. This sentinel is
unambiguous for text parsing: a real NUL in the source would be returned
exactly the same way, but in practice MuDSL's parsers operate on source
text where NUL does not occur. A parser that wishes to distinguish ``NUL
in input'' from ``past end'' should call
\texttt{eof(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} first.

\begin{code}
bool at_digit(dsl::ParsecInput &in) {
  return !in.eof() && in.peek() >= '0' && in.peek() <= '9';
}
\end{code}

\texttt{eof(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} returns \texttt{true} when
\texttt{pos \textgreater{}\allowbreak{}= source.\allowbreak{}size(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}.
It is the recommended guard before any
\texttt{peek(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}-based decision where the
distinction between end-of-input and a real \texttt{'\textbackslash{}0'}
matters. The built-in \texttt{satisfy} parser, documented below, follows
exactly this pattern.

\texttt{pos} is a public member, so a parser may inspect it directly to
record where a token began. The \texttt{get\_\allowbreak{}span} member
uses two positions to extract a substring of the source, which is how
token-style parsers recover lexeme text after consuming characters.

The cursor position is also the canonical location used in error
reports. Every \texttt{ParseError} carries a \texttt{pos} field of type
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}size\_\allowbreak{}t}, and the
convention throughout the library is that this position refers to the
same index space as \texttt{ParsecInput:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pos}.
When a parser fails without consuming, the error position is typically
the cursor position at entry.

\section{Consuming Input and Capturing
Spans}\label{ch23-consuming-input-and-capturing-spans}

\texttt{consume(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} is the primary mutator. It
returns the character at the current position and advances \texttt{pos}
by one, or returns \texttt{'\textbackslash{}0'} and leaves \texttt{pos}
unchanged if already at end-of-input. A parser that matches a single
character therefore looks like: peek to test, then consume to accept.

\begin{code}
auto p = dsl::parser([](dsl::ParsecInput &in) -> dsl::ExpectedResult<char> {
  if (in.peek() == ';')
    return in.consume();
  return dsl::fail_expected<char>(in, "semicolon");
});
\end{code}

The expression
\texttt{in.\allowbreak{}consume(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} returns a
\texttt{char}, which is implicitly converted to
\texttt{ExpectedResult\textless{}\allowbreak{}char\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
via the constructor
\texttt{ExpectedResult(\allowbreak{}T)\allowbreak{}}. This is the most
common shape for a primitive parser: test a predicate on
\texttt{peek(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}, return
\texttt{consume(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} on success, return
\texttt{fail\_\allowbreak{}expected} on failure.

For multi-character tokens, a parser consumes in a loop and then calls
\texttt{get\_\allowbreak{}span(\allowbreak{}start)\allowbreak{}} to
recover the matched text. \texttt{get\_\allowbreak{}span} returns the
substring of \texttt{source} from \texttt{start} up to the current
\texttt{pos}.

\begin{code}
auto ident = dsl::parser([](dsl::ParsecInput &in) -> dsl::ExpectedResult<std::string> {
  std::size_t start = in.pos;
  while (!in.eof() && (std::isalpha(static_cast<unsigned char>(in.peek()))))
    in.consume();
  if (in.pos == start)
    return dsl::fail_expected<std::string>(in, "identifier");
  return std::string(in.get_span(start));
});
\end{code}

Because \texttt{pos} is public, a parser may also advance it directly
when bulk movement is more convenient than repeated
\texttt{consume(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} calls. The combinators
themselves use direct \texttt{pos} assignment for save and restore,
e.g.~\texttt{in.\allowbreak{}pos = save;}. Direct assignment is
appropriate for backtracking; it is not appropriate as a substitute for
\texttt{consume(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} in ordinary
single-character matching, because the return value of
\texttt{consume(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} is frequently the value the
parser wants to return.

\section{ParseError: Structured
Failure}\label{ch23-parseerror-structured-failure}

A parser failure is not an exception. It is a value of type
\texttt{ParseError}, which records where the failure happened, how
committed it is, and what the parser expected to find.

\begin{code}
enum class ParseFailureKind
{
  Soft,
  Committed
};

struct ParseError
{
  std::size_t pos{ 0 };
  ParseFailureKind kind{ ParseFailureKind::Soft };
  std::vector<std::string> expected{};
};
\end{code}

The \texttt{pos} field is the source position associated with the
failure. The \texttt{kind} field classifies the failure as either
\texttt{Soft} or \texttt{Committed}, a distinction explained in the next
section. The \texttt{expected} field is a vector of human-readable
labels describing what the parser would have accepted.

\texttt{ParseError} is default-constructible to a soft failure at
position zero with no expected labels. This default is used as the
starting accumulator in combinators that merge errors across
alternatives. A default-constructed \texttt{ParseError} is not itself a
meaningful diagnostic --- it is a neutral element for the merge
operation.

The \texttt{expected} vector is a sequence rather than a set, and the
merge helper preserves insertion order while suppressing duplicates.
This means the order in which alternatives are tried is reflected in the
order of labels in a reported error, which is usually the order in which
the grammar lists them.

Errors accumulate through the free function
\texttt{merge\_\allowbreak{}error}, which combines two
\texttt{ParseError} values into one.

\begin{code}
inline void
merge_error (ParseError &dst, const ParseError &src)
{
  if (src.pos > dst.pos)
    {
      dst = src;
      return;
    }
  if (src.pos < dst.pos)
    return;
  if (src.kind == ParseFailureKind::Committed)
    dst.kind = ParseFailureKind::Committed;
  for (const auto &label : src.expected)
    {
      if (std::find (dst.expected.begin (), dst.expected.end (), label)
          == dst.expected.end ())
        dst.expected.push_back (label);
    }
}
\end{code}

The merge rule is positional. The error that progressed furthest wins,
on the principle that the deepest failure is the most informative. When
two errors share the same position, their expected-label sets are
unioned and the result is \texttt{Committed} if either input was
\texttt{Committed}. This is the mechanism by which a sequence of
alternatives produces a single diagnostic listing every acceptable token
at the furthest point of failure.

\section{ParseFailureKind: Soft vs
Committed}\label{ch23-parsefailurekind-soft-vs-committed}

\texttt{ParseFailureKind\allowbreak{}} distinguishes two kinds of
failure. A \texttt{Soft} failure is one where the parser tried
something, it did not work out, and backtracking is acceptable --- the
caller may legitimately try another alternative. A \texttt{Committed}
failure is one where the parser had already consumed input and committed
to a branch, so backtracking is not appropriate.

This distinction is what prevents the alternative combinator \texttt{|}
from silently swallowing failures that should be fatal. When
\texttt{a | b} is evaluated and \texttt{a} fails softly, the combinator
restores the cursor and tries \texttt{b}. When \texttt{a} fails with
\texttt{Committed}, the combinator returns the failure immediately
without trying \texttt{b}.

The sequence combinator \texttt{\&} promotes a soft failure to committed
when the first operand had already consumed input before the second
operand failed. The relevant lines in the header capture the rule
precisely: if the sequence consumed input and the second operand failed,
the failure kind becomes \texttt{Committed}. This prevents a later
alternative from recovering after a partial match that the grammar
intended to be committed.

The repetition combinator \texttt{*} treats a committed failure inside
the loop as fatal to the whole repetition, while a soft failure simply
terminates the loop and returns what was collected. The
\texttt{optional} combinator similarly treats a committed failure as
fatal and a soft failure as ``no match, but proceed.''

Authors of hand-written primitive parsers should default to
\texttt{Soft} failures, which is what
\texttt{fail\_\allowbreak{}expected} produces when called with no kind
argument. Producing a \texttt{Committed} failure from a primitive is
rare; commitment is usually a property introduced by combinators that
have observed consumption.

\section{ExpectedResult: Value and
Diagnostics}\label{ch23-expectedresult-value-and-diagnostics}

\texttt{ExpectedResult\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
is the return type of every parser. It pairs an optional parsed value
with a \texttt{ParseError}, so that even a successful parse carries
diagnostic context and even a failed parse carries structured
information about what went wrong.

\begin{code}
template <typename T> struct ExpectedResult
{
  using value_type = T;

  std::optional<T> value{};
  ParseError error{};

  ExpectedResult () = default;
  ExpectedResult (std::nullopt_t) : value (std::nullopt) {}
  ExpectedResult (const T &v) : value (v) {}
  ExpectedResult (T &&v) : value (std::move (v)) {}

  static ExpectedResult
  failure (std::size_t pos, ParseFailureKind kind = ParseFailureKind::Soft,
           std::vector<std::string> expected = {})
  {
    ExpectedResult out{};
    out.error = ParseError{ pos, kind, std::move (expected) };
    return out;
  }

  explicit operator bool () const { return value.has_value (); }
  T &operator* () { return *value; }
  const T &operator* () const { return *value; }
};
\end{code}

The type has a \texttt{value\_\allowbreak{}type} alias, which
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}parser} uses to deduce \texttt{T}
from a lambda's return type. The \texttt{value} member is an
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
empty on failure and populated on success. The \texttt{error} member is
always present --- a default-constructed \texttt{ParseError} on success,
a meaningful one on failure.

The constructors make success ergonomic. A parser that has a value
simply returns it, and the implicit conversion from \texttt{T} to
\texttt{ExpectedResult\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
does the right thing. A parser that has consumed a character can write
\texttt{return in.\allowbreak{}consume(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{};} and
the \texttt{char} is wrapped. The \texttt{failure} static factory is the
counterpart for constructing an explicit failure with a position, a
kind, and a list of expected labels.

The truthiness test is \texttt{explicit operato\allowbreak{}r bool},
which queries
\texttt{value.\allowbreak{}has\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}.
This means \texttt{if (\allowbreak{}r)\allowbreak{}} checks success,
while \texttt{if (\allowbreak{}!r)\allowbreak{}} checks failure. The
dereference operator \texttt{*} returns a reference to the contained
value; dereferencing a failed result is undefined behavior because the
optional is empty, so callers should always check first.

Note the design decision: the \texttt{error} field is not cleared on
success. A parser constructed as
\texttt{ExpectedResult\{s\allowbreak{}ome\_\allowbreak{}value\}} has a
default-constructed \texttt{ParseError} (position zero, soft, empty
expected). The combinators do not rely on the error field of a
successful result; they accumulate errors only from failed sub-results.
The field's presence on success is a consequence of keeping the type
simple rather than a tagged union.

\section{Inspecting a Result}\label{ch23-inspecting-a-result}

Caller code typically inspects an \texttt{ExpectedResult} in one of two
ways. The first is the boolean test, used when only success or failure
matters.

\begin{code}
auto r = some_parser(in);
if (r) {
  use(*r);
} else {
  report(r.error);
}
\end{code}

The second is direct inspection of the \texttt{value} and \texttt{error}
members, used when the diagnostic must be preserved regardless of
outcome.

\begin{code}
auto r = some_parser(in);
if (r.value) {
  use(*r.value);
}
// r.error is always available for reporting
\end{code}

The two styles are equivalent because \texttt{operator bool} is exactly
\texttt{value.\allowbreak{}has\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}.
The first is preferred for its brevity; the second is useful when a
function wants to forward both the value and the error onward without
branching.

The dereference operator is provided in both const and non-const
overloads. The non-const overload returns \texttt{T\&}, allowing
in-place mutation of the parsed value before it is consumed by the next
combinator. The const overload returns \texttt{const T\&}. Neither
performs a copy beyond what the optional already owns.

\section{The Parser Model}\label{ch23-the-parser-model}

\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is
the type that wraps a parser callable. It is a struct with a single
member, a \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}function} holding the
parse logic.

\begin{code}
template <typename T> struct Parser
{
  std::function<ExpectedResult<T> (ParsecInput &)> parse;
  ExpectedResult<T>
  operator() (ParsecInput &input) const
  {
    return parse (input);
  }
};
\end{code}

The \texttt{parse} member holds the callable. The
\texttt{operator(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} forwards to it, so a
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is
itself callable: given a \texttt{ParsecInput\&}, it returns an
\texttt{ExpectedResult\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
This is what makes parsers composable --- every combinator receives
\texttt{Parser} objects and produces a new \texttt{Parser} whose
\texttt{parse} member captures the originals by value.

\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is
copyable because \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}function} is
copyable. The combinators exploit this by capturing parsers by value in
their lambdas, which means a composed parser owns copies of its
sub-parsers. There is no shared state between a parser and its copies;
each invocation is independent.

The library does not expose a concept named \texttt{Parser} constraining
the callable. The model is structural: anything that can be stored in a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}function\textless{}\allowbreak{}ExpectedResult\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}ParsecInput\&)\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and invoked with a \texttt{ParsecInput\&} is a parser. The
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}parser} factory is the canonical
way to lift a lambda into a
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.

A \texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
may be default-constructed, leaving \texttt{parse} empty. Calling a
default-constructed parser throws
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}bad\_\allowbreak{}function\_\allowbreak{}call},
the standard behavior of an empty
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}function}. The library's own
factories always initialize \texttt{parse}, so this case arises only
from user error.

\section{The parser() Factory}\label{ch23-the-parser-factory}

The free function template
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}parser} is the bridge from a raw
lambda to a
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}. It
deduces \texttt{T} from the lambda's return type so that the caller does
not have to spell it out.

\begin{code}
template <typename Fn>
auto
parser (Fn &&fn)
{
  using Ret = decltype (fn (std::declval<ParsecInput &> ()));
  using T = typename Ret::value_type;
  return Parser<T>{ std::forward<Fn> (fn) };
}
\end{code}

The factory requires that the lambda's return type be an
\texttt{ExpectedResult\textless{}\allowbreak{}U\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
for some \texttt{U}, because it extracts
\texttt{value\_\allowbreak{}type} from it. A lambda returning
\texttt{char} or \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string} directly
will not compile; the caller must wrap the value in an
\texttt{ExpectedResult} (or rely on the implicit conversion, which works
only when the return type is exactly
\texttt{ExpectedResult\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}).

In practice, the implicit conversion makes the common case pleasant. A
lambda whose declared return type is
\texttt{ExpectedResult\textless{}\allowbreak{}char\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
may return a bare \texttt{char}, and the converting constructor takes
care of the wrapping. The declared return type is what the factory
inspects, so it must be written out.

\begin{code}
auto digit = dsl::parser([](dsl::ParsecInput &in) -> dsl::ExpectedResult<char> {
  if (!in.eof() && in.peek() >= '0' && in.peek() <= '9')
    return in.consume();
  return dsl::fail_expected<char>(in, "digit");
});
\end{code}

The factory perfect-forwards the lambda into the
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}function}. For capturing lambdas
this means the captures are moved or copied into the function object
according to the lambda's own copy/move behavior. A parser that captures
heavy state by value will be expensive to copy; the combinators copy
parsers freely, so authors of stateful primitives should keep captured
state small.

\section{fail\_expected and labeled}\label{ch23-fail_expected-and-labeled}

Two helper templates make primitive parsers easier to write and easier
to diagnose. The first, \texttt{fail\_\allowbreak{}expected}, constructs
a failure result from a cursor and a label.

\begin{code}
template <typename T>
ExpectedResult<T>
fail_expected (const ParsecInput &in, std::string label,
               ParseFailureKind kind = ParseFailureKind::Soft)
{
  return ExpectedResult<T>::failure (in.pos, kind, { std::move (label) });
}
\end{code}

It reads the cursor's current position and produces a soft failure by
default with a single expected label. It is the standard way for a
primitive parser to report that it did not match. The position is taken
from the cursor at the point of failure, which is why primitives should
not advance the cursor before deciding to fail.

The second helper, \texttt{labeled}, wraps a parser so that any failure
it produces is reported under a single human-readable label rather than
the labels contributed by its internals.

\begin{code}
template <typename T>
Parser<T>
labeled (const Parser<T> &p, std::string expected)
{
  return parser ([p, expected = std::move (expected)] (
                     ParsecInput &in) -> ExpectedResult<T> {
    auto r = p (in);
    if (!r)
      r.error.expected = { expected };
    return r;
  });
}
\end{code}

\texttt{labeled} does not change success or failure outcomes; it
rewrites the \texttt{expected} list of a failed result to a single
label. This is how
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}ch(\allowbreak{}'a')\allowbreak{}}
reports its failure as expecting \texttt{"a"} regardless of how its
inner callable is structured. The built-in \texttt{ch} and
\texttt{satisfy} primitives both wrap their implementations in
\texttt{labeled}.

\section{The Built-In ch and satisfy
Primitives}\label{ch23-the-built-in-ch-and-satisfy-primitives}

The header ships two complete primitive parsers that illustrate every
concept introduced so far. \texttt{ch} matches a single literal
character.

\begin{code}
inline Parser<char>
ch (char c)
{
  return labeled (
      parser ([c] (ParsecInput &in) -> ExpectedResult<char> {
        if (in.peek () == c)
          return in.consume ();
        return fail_expected<char> (in, std::string (1, c));
      }),
      std::string (1, c));
}
\end{code}

The structure is canonical: peek, test, consume on success,
\texttt{fail\_\allowbreak{}expected} on failure, and wrap the whole in
\texttt{labeled} so the diagnostic reads as the character itself. The
label is the one-character string form of \texttt{c}.

\texttt{satisfy} generalizes \texttt{ch} to any predicate.

\begin{code}
inline Parser<char>
satisfy (std::function<bool (char)> pred, std::string label)
{
  return labeled (
      parser ([pred = std::move (pred), label] (
                  ParsecInput &in) -> ExpectedResult<char> {
        if (!in.eof () && pred (in.peek ()))
          return in.consume ();
        return fail_expected<char> (in, label);
      }),
      std::move (label));
}
\end{code}

Note the guard \texttt{!in.\allowbreak{}eof(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
before invoking the predicate. This is the pattern recommended earlier:
check end-of-input first, so that a predicate never has to interpret the
\texttt{'\textbackslash{}0'} sentinel as a real character. The label is
forwarded to \texttt{labeled} so that failures of \texttt{satisfy}
report the predicate's name, not the anonymous internals.

These two primitives are the model to follow when writing custom
parsers. A custom parser that returns a richer type than \texttt{char}
follows the same shape but constructs its
\texttt{ExpectedResult\textless{}\allowbreak{}U\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
from whatever value it builds.

\section{Running a Parser:
run\_parser}\label{ch23-running-a-parser-run_parser}

A parser is driven by passing it a \texttt{ParsecInput}. The free
function
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}run\_\allowbreak{}parser} is the
high-level entry point that constructs the cursor from a
\texttt{string\_\allowbreak{}view}, runs the parser, and applies a final
end-of-input check.

\begin{code}
template <typename T>
struct ParseOutcome
{
  std::optional<T> value{};
  ParseError error{};
};

template <typename T>
ParseOutcome<T>
run_parser (const Parser<T> &p, std::string_view source)
{
  ParsecInput in{ source, 0 };
  auto r = p (in);
  if (!r)
    return { std::nullopt, r.error };
  if (!in.eof ())
    {
      ParseError trailing{ in.pos, ParseFailureKind::Committed,
                           { "<end-of-input>" } };
      return { std::nullopt, trailing };
    }
  return { std::move (r.value), {} };
}
\end{code}

\texttt{run\_\allowbreak{}parser} returns a
\texttt{ParseOutcome\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
which is structurally similar to
\texttt{ExpectedResult\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
but is a distinct type used only at the top level. Its \texttt{value} is
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and its \texttt{error} is \texttt{ParseError}, the same members as
\texttt{ExpectedResult} but in a separate type so that top-level results
are not confused with intermediate parser results.

Three outcomes are possible. If the parser fails, the outcome carries
\texttt{nullopt} and the parser's error. If the parser succeeds but the
cursor is not at end-of-input, the outcome is a committed failure with
the label
\texttt{"\textless{}\allowbreak{}end-of-input\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}"},
signaling trailing input that the parser did not consume. If the parser
succeeds and the cursor is at end-of-input, the outcome carries the
parsed value and an empty error.

The trailing-input check is important. Without it, a parser that matches
only a prefix would silently succeed and discard the rest of the source.
By treating non-consumed trailing input as a committed failure,
\texttt{run\_\allowbreak{}parser} enforces the expectation that a
top-level parse accounts for the entire input. Parsers intended to
consume a prefix should be combined with a recovery or continuation
parser rather than run directly through
\texttt{run\_\allowbreak{}parser}.

\section{A First Parser: Single
Character}\label{ch23-a-first-parser-single-character}

The simplest end-to-end example runs
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}ch} on a one-character input.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

int main() {
  auto p = dsl::ch('a');
  auto out = dsl::run_parser(p, "a");
  if (out.value)
    std::cout << "matched: " << *out.value << "\n";
  else
    std::cout << "error at " << out.error.pos << "\n";
}
\end{code}

The parser \texttt{ch(\allowbreak{}'a')\allowbreak{}} succeeds on the
input \texttt{"a"}, consumes the single character, leaves the cursor at
end-of-input, and \texttt{run\_\allowbreak{}parser} returns an outcome
whose \texttt{value} holds \texttt{'a'}. The
\texttt{if (\allowbreak{}out.\allowbreak{}value)\allowbreak{}} test
distinguishes success from failure, and \texttt{*out.\allowbreak{}value}
extracts the matched character.

This example is the minimal pattern for using the parser core: build a
parser, run it with \texttt{run\_\allowbreak{}parser}, and inspect the
\texttt{ParseOutcome}. Everything else --- composition, diagnostics,
custom primitives --- builds on this skeleton.

\section{Inspecting Success and
Failure}\label{ch23-inspecting-success-and-failure}

Changing the input to one that does not match exercises the failure
path.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

int main() {
  auto p = dsl::ch('a');
  auto out = dsl::run_parser(p, "b");
  if (!out.value) {
    std::cout << "error at position " << out.error.pos << "\n";
    std::cout << "expected:";
    for (const auto &label : out.error.expected)
      std::cout << " " << label;
    std::cout << "\n";
  }
}
\end{code}

Here \texttt{ch(\allowbreak{}'a')\allowbreak{}} peeks \texttt{'b'},
fails to match, and returns a soft failure at position zero with
expected label \texttt{"a"}. The cursor is not advanced.
\texttt{run\_\allowbreak{}parser} sees the failure and returns it as the
outcome's error. The diagnostic printed is
\texttt{error at positio\allowbreak{}n 0} followed by
\texttt{expected:\allowbreak{} a}.

This is the shape of every diagnostic produced by the core: a position
and a list of expected labels. The combinators of Chapter 24 will
produce richer diagnostics by merging errors from multiple alternatives,
but the format at the top level is always the same.

\section{Building Literal Parsers}\label{ch23-building-literal-parsers}

A multi-character literal is a sequence of single-character parsers. The
sequence combinator \texttt{\&} is documented in Chapter 24; here it is
used only to demonstrate that composed parsers run through the same
\texttt{run\_\allowbreak{}parser} entry point.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

int main() {
  auto p = dsl::ch('a') & dsl::ch('b');
  auto out = dsl::run_parser(p, "ab");
  std::cout << (out.value.has_value() ? "ok" : "err") << "\n";
}
\end{code}

The sequence
\texttt{ch(\allowbreak{}'a')\allowbreak{} \& ch(\allowbreak{}'b')\allowbreak{}}
produces a
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pair\textless{}\allowbreak{}char,\allowbreak{}char\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
On input \texttt{"ab"} it succeeds, consuming both characters. On input
\texttt{"ax"} it fails at position one with expected label \texttt{"b"}.
The \texttt{run\_\allowbreak{}parser} outcome's \texttt{value} holds the
pair on success and is empty on failure.

A hand-written literal parser that returns the matched text rather than
a pair of characters follows the
loop-and-\texttt{get\_\allowbreak{}span} pattern shown earlier in the
identifier example. The combinator-based approach is usually clearer and
is the recommended style once Chapter 24's primitives are available.

\section{A Custom Predicate Parser}\label{ch23-a-custom-predicate-parser}

The \texttt{satisfy} primitive handles most character-class matching
without requiring a hand-written parser. A vowel matcher is a one-liner.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

int main() {
  auto vowel = dsl::satisfy(
      [](char c) { return c == 'a' || c == 'e' || c == 'i'
                        || c == 'o' || c == 'u'; },
      "vowel");
  auto out = dsl::run_parser(vowel, "e");
  if (out.value)
    std::cout << "matched vowel " << *out.value << "\n";
}
\end{code}

The predicate is wrapped in a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}function\textless{}\allowbreak{}bool(\allowbreak{}char)\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
so a stateless lambda converts implicitly. The label \texttt{"vowel"} is
what \texttt{labeled} will substitute into any failure's expected list.
Running this on input \texttt{"e"} succeeds and reports the matched
character; running it on \texttt{"x"} fails at position zero with
\texttt{expected:\allowbreak{} vowel}.

This example demonstrates the division of labor between the core and the
primitives. The core provides \texttt{ParsecInput},
\texttt{ExpectedResult}, and \texttt{Parser}; the primitives like
\texttt{satisfy} provide convenient parameterized parsers built on the
core; the combinators of Chapter 24 compose primitives into grammars.

\section{Driving a Parser by Hand}\label{ch23-driving-a-parser-by-hand}

\texttt{run\_\allowbreak{}parser} is convenient but opinionated: it
requires the parser to consume the entire input. For cases where a
prefix parse is intended, a caller may construct a \texttt{ParsecInput}
directly and invoke the parser.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

int main() {
  dsl::ParsecInput in{ "abc", 0 };
  auto p = dsl::ch('a');
  auto r = p(in);
  if (r) {
    std::cout << "matched " << *r << ", remaining: "
              << in.source.substr(in.pos) << "\n";
  }
}
\end{code}

Here the parser consumes only the first character. The remaining input
\texttt{"bc"} is available as
\texttt{in.\allowbreak{}source.\allowbreak{}substr(\allowbreak{}in.\allowbreak{}pos)\allowbreak{}}.
The result \texttt{r} is the raw
\texttt{ExpectedResult\textless{}\allowbreak{}char\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}};
no \texttt{ParseOutcome} is constructed because
\texttt{run\_\allowbreak{}parser} was bypassed. This pattern is useful
when a parser is embedded in a larger system that manages its own input
framing, such as the token-stream integration sketched in
\texttt{examples/\allowbreak{}15-tokenize-pars\allowbreak{}e-integration.\allowbreak{}cpp}.

Direct construction of \texttt{ParsecInput} is also how recursive
descent with embedded combinator parsers is implemented: the outer
driver maintains the cursor and calls sub-parsers on it, threading the
same \texttt{ParsecInput\&} through the recursion.

\section{Value Semantics and
Copyability}\label{ch23-value-semantics-and-copyability}

Parsers are values. A
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is
copyable, and copying copies the
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}function} it holds. The
combinators capture parsers by value in the lambdas they construct,
which means a composed parser owns its sub-parsers outright. There is no
need to manage lifetimes, no shared pointers, no \texttt{this} captured
into a combinator that outlives its origin.

The price of this simplicity is that a parser with large captured state
is expensive to copy, and the combinators copy freely. In practice,
primitives capture at most a \texttt{char}, a predicate, and a label, so
the cost is negligible. A parser that needs to capture a large table
should consider storing the table in a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}shared\_\allowbreak{}ptr} and
capturing the shared pointer, so that copies of the parser share the
table rather than duplicating it.

\texttt{ParsecInput}, by contrast, is not passed by value. It is passed
by reference, because it is the mutable state of the parse in progress.
Copying a \texttt{ParsecInput} would snapshot the cursor, which is
occasionally useful for speculative parsing but is not what the ordinary
parser signature expects. The combinators that need to snapshot copy
only the \texttt{pos} member (\texttt{auto save = in.\allowbreak{}pos;})
and restore it (\texttt{in.\allowbreak{}pos = save;}), which is cheaper
than copying the whole struct and clearer in intent.

\texttt{ExpectedResult\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
is a value type and is returned by value from every parser. Moves are
used where possible: the \texttt{failure} factory moves the
expected-label vector, and \texttt{run\_\allowbreak{}parser} moves the
final value into the outcome. For large value types, move semantics keep
the cost of returning an \texttt{ExpectedResult} low.

\section{Why Combinators}\label{ch23-why-combinators}

The core types documented in this chapter are sufficient to write any
parser by hand, but writing parsers by hand is tedious. The combinators
of Chapter 24 exist to factor out the common patterns: sequencing two
parsers, choosing between alternatives, repeating a parser zero or more
times, and making a parser optional.

Each combinator is a template function that takes one or two
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
objects and returns a new
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}U\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}. The
implementation of each combinator is a lambda that runs its operands
with appropriate save-and-restore of the cursor and appropriate merging
of errors. The result is a parser in exactly the sense of this chapter
--- a callable conforming to the
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
model --- so combinators compose with primitives and with each other
without distinction.

The forward references in this chapter --- \texttt{\&} for sequence,
\texttt{|} for alternative, \texttt{*} for repetition, \texttt{optional}
for optionality --- are documented fully in Chapter 24. The
error-merging behavior described here through
\texttt{merge\_\allowbreak{}error} is the mechanism the combinators use
internally to produce the diagnostics that
\texttt{run\_\allowbreak{}parser} ultimately returns. Chapter 25 covers
the diagnostic surface in more detail, including how \texttt{labeled}
and the committed/soft distinction shape the messages a user sees.

\section{Pitfalls}\label{ch23-pitfalls}

Several pitfalls await the unwary author of hand-written parsers. The
first and most common is forgetting to advance the cursor on success. A
parser that returns a value without consuming input will, when combined
with the repetition combinator \texttt{*}, loop forever: the combinator
keeps trying the parser at the same position, keeps succeeding, and
never makes progress. Every primitive that succeeds must advance
\texttt{pos} by at least one, or must be combined in a context that
guarantees progress.

The second pitfall is advancing the cursor and then failing. A primitive
that consumes input and then decides it cannot complete the match should
restore \texttt{pos} before returning a soft failure, otherwise a later
alternative will start from the wrong position. The combinators handle
this for their operands, but a hand-written primitive that consumes
speculatively must handle it itself. The
\texttt{try\_\allowbreak{}parse} wrapper, documented in Chapter 24,
exists to restore the cursor on failure; it is the recommended tool for
speculative consumption.

The third pitfall is treating \texttt{ExpectedResult}'s \texttt{error}
field as meaningful on success. On a successful parse the \texttt{error}
is default-constructed --- position zero, soft, empty expected --- and
carries no information. Code that forwards an \texttt{ExpectedResult}
should not assume the error is populated; it should branch on
\texttt{operator bool} first, as the examples in this chapter do.

The fourth pitfall is expecting parser failures to throw. They do not. A
parser that encounters malformed input returns a failure value, and the
caller --- typically a combinator or \texttt{run\_\allowbreak{}parser}
--- inspects that value. There is no \texttt{try}/\texttt{catch} around
parser invocation, and the library does not use exceptions for control
flow within the parser core. This aligns with the error-flow discipline
of Chapter 22's
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.

The fifth pitfall is letting the source string's storage expire before
the parse completes. \texttt{ParsecInput} holds a
\texttt{string\_\allowbreak{}view}, not a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string}. If the caller constructs
a cursor from a temporary
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string}, the cursor's
\texttt{source} dangles after the temporary is destroyed. The caller
must keep the underlying storage alive for the duration of the parse,
typically by binding the source to a named variable before constructing
the cursor or calling \texttt{run\_\allowbreak{}parser}.

The sixth pitfall is conflating
\texttt{ParseOutcome\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
with
\texttt{ExpectedResult\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
They have the same shape but are different types.
\texttt{ExpectedResult} is the intermediate result produced by every
parser; \texttt{ParseOutcome} is the top-level result produced by
\texttt{run\_\allowbreak{}parser}. Functions that accept one will not
accept the other, and the two should not be confused when designing APIs
around the parser core.

\section{Summary}\label{ch23-summary}

This chapter documented the parser-combinator substrate of MuDSL.
\texttt{ParsecInput} is the mutable input cursor, holding a
\texttt{string\_\allowbreak{}view} source and a \texttt{pos} index, with
\texttt{peek}, \texttt{eof}, \texttt{consume}, and
\texttt{get\_\allowbreak{}span} operations. \texttt{ParseError} is the
structured failure record, carrying a position, a soft-or-committed
kind, and a list of expected labels, merged by
\texttt{merge\_\allowbreak{}error} according to furthest-position-wins
semantics.
\texttt{ExpectedResult\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
pairs an
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
value with a \texttt{ParseError}, serving as the universal return type
of parsers.
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
wraps a \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}function} and is itself
callable, and the \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}parser} factory
lifts lambdas into this model.
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}run\_\allowbreak{}parser} is the
top-level entry point, seeding a cursor from a
\texttt{string\_\allowbreak{}view}, running the parser, enforcing
end-of-input, and returning a
\texttt{ParseOutcome\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.

The core is deliberately minimal: it provides value-semantic parsers,
structured best-effort diagnostics, and a clean separation between input
state and parse results. Failures are values, not exceptions, consistent
with the error-flow discipline of Chapter 22. The combinators that turn
these primitives into a grammar --- sequence, alternative, repetition,
optional --- are the subject of Chapter 24. Diagnostics, semantic
actions, and the broader \texttt{run\_\allowbreak{}parser} story are
covered in Chapter 25. The PEG layers in Chapter 27 and Chapter 28 build
specialized grammars on top of the same core.
